  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 152 AM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nFriday...Isolated to scattered storms (20-40% coverage) are \nback on the table as a surface trough moves through the area. \nLimited instability due to the lack of prolonged return flow and\nWAA should dampen the severe threat with these storms, but the \nnorthwesterly flow aloft does put the shear vector perpendicular\nto the front, leaving the door open to a possible MCS \ndevelopment as noted in the SPC's Day 4-8 Convective Outlook. \nBy Friday night, the boundary clears the CWA as another weak \nhigh pressure moves in.\n\nSaturday...A rinse-and-repeat of Thursday is likely on Saturday\nas convergence aloft on the backside of Friday's shortwave \ntrough and low-level CAA keep things dry. Highs and lows should \nbe seasonable, but once again, these pleasant conditions will \nnot last.\n\nSunday-Tuesday...There is a lot of uncertainty in PoPs heading into \nearly next week as persistent west-northwesterly flow aloft prevents \nany significant moisture advection into the region. What is a little \nmore certain is the shift in low-level flow. Another weak shortwave \nis forecast to deepen a low pressure on the leeward side of the\nNorthern Rockies and move eastward towards Iowa and Minnesota \non Monday. General consensus among ensemble models is a \nsoutherly wind shift which will kick off a warming trend Sunday \nand into early next week with highs likely (60-80% chance per \n04.12z LREF guidance) in the mid-80s to low 90s. Any residual \nboundary left across the region on Tuesday will also play a huge\nrole on the northward expansion of the heat.",
